# ELM format

Canonical reference: `docs/All elementary functions from a single operator.html`.

## Operator

```
elm(x, y) = exp(x) - ln(y)
```

Paired with the constant `1` (`ln(1) = 0`), this generates the scientific-calculator basis (arithmetic, elementary functions, and constants such as `e`, `pi`, `i`).

## Textual grammar

Closed programs are a binary tree of `elm` and `1`. Parameterized programs also allow `$VARNAME` leaves (bound by `elm run`):

```
S → 1 | $VARNAME | elm(S, S)
```

A `.elm` file is ELM text. Without variables it contains only `elm(...)` and `1`.

## Identities (from the paper; use `elm` not `eml`)

- `e = elm(1, 1)`
- `exp(x) = elm(x, 1)`
- `ln(z) = elm(1, elm(elm(1, z), 1))`

Further rewrites (arithmetic, trig, constants, …) come from that paper. Prefer its identities over ad-hoc equivalents unless a later optimization pass says otherwise.

## Interpreter

The same executable interprets ELM by evaluating the `elm` tree over complex numbers. Do not require LLVM / .NET / JVM to interpret.
